prompt sha256: df7943ac4e76e289f046a66fd9743734349143ccbe6059130a02e67be939b434

Blue lane 3 of 3 for topic: "the seat prompt contract".
Read the frontier hypotheses from the RECORD — the `inquiries` projection — where each carries an id and its hypothesis.
Research your assigned slice to saturation per your constitution (spend at least one search in five on disconfirming evidence; note each source inline with its URL, title, and access date).
Your assigned METHOD: local-repo critical-stance (audit the subject artifacts/codebase directly; trust nothing secondhand) — work primarily through this method's source class; take hypothesis 3 first, then breadth.
YOUR SOURCES, IN PROSE (not footnotes): a lane writes its part of the report and does not cite — record each intended source as prose beside the claim (its URL, title, and access date), and the program behind each figure by its path under scratch/, so the synthesizer can record each once with the tool.
Do NOT mint footnote labels; citations are tool-managed at synthesis and a hand-typed label backs nothing.
YOUR PART HOLDS THE REPORT'S OWN SECTIONS ONLY, each under a `## ` heading of its own: no title, no TL;DR, Catechism or open questions, and no list of sources — the synthesizer writes those, and a part carrying one is refused.
Write your part of the report, in full, to research/2026-01-01_golden/blue/candidates/blue-lane-3.md, then record it — the act your seat's help lists for your part of the report, which takes that file and no other.
Record it ONCE; where it refuses, the file stands for you to fix and record again.
What the synthesizer makes of it is the synthesizer's: once your part is recorded, your sitting is done.
INQUIRIES: each approach the research took or weighed is on the record once, as an inquiry — the question it asks, with the hypothesis that would be true if it paid off kept apart from the question, and later with the fate it ACTUALLY had, what was done and what was found.
Record the fate the approach really had; what each fate means is on the page of the act that records it.
NO FATE IS OWED AN ENTRY.
A sitting that weighed nothing and set it down has no declined inquiry, and that record is complete: an approach thought up, or run, so that there is one to decline is a false entry, and a declined inquiry whose reason reports a result is one too.
The hypothesis is what makes the account honest, and a dead end you really reached is worth recording: one on the record is one the next run does not re-walk.
THE TOOL WRITES THE REPORT'S ACCOUNT OF THEM, NOT YOU.
At assembly it composes three sections of the report from the inquiries on the record: Research areas holds each inquiry that is proposed, underway, confirmed or refuted, Future research directions each one deferred, and Alternatives considered each one declined or abandoned.
That gives the reader what was run, what was deferred and what was set aside as three things, not one list of winners.
You write none of those three headings in the report, and no list of the approaches considered and their fates under any other heading: a report that carries one of those headings is refused, and a hand copy elsewhere is a second account nothing checks against the record.
The question you propose, its hypothesis, and the method and reason you give with its fate ARE PRINTED IN THE REPORT word for word, each under its own label, so write them as research prose for a reader of the SUBJECT — the question the inquiry asks and what was found about it, never the steps you took.
THE QUESTION HOLDS NO PREDICTED RESULT: what you expect to find is the hypothesis, and a question that states a result is printed as the question whatever the research then finds.
Recording each approach with the fate it had is what makes "explore the alternatives" checkable instead of self-attested.
SPEED: ONE MESSAGE IS ONE ROUND-TRIP, whatever it carries.
Five independent tool calls in ONE message cost one round-trip; the same five in five messages cost five, and a round-trip is seconds.
So batch every call that does not need a prior call's OUTPUT as its input: all your projections together, independent fetches together, several file reads together.
Serialize only where the second call cannot be written until the first has answered.
If you are about to send a message with one tool call in it, ask what else you already know you will need.
READ a file with the file-reading tool (offset/limit), never sed/awk/head/tail/cat through the shell: a shell spawn costs 10-100x a native read and buys nothing.
SEARCH with the search tool your session offers; where it offers none, or the tool refuses a path under the run directory ("Path does not exist" — a KNOWN HARNESS LIMIT, W1.11, do NOT log it as friction), search through the shell (grep, ls) — one command that finds the lines, never a whole file dumped to be read by eye.
SEAT_ID: blue-lane-3.
RUN DIRECTORY: research/2026-01-01_golden.
THE RECORD TOOL IS THE CONTRACT: every act of this seat happens through "/opt/feov/bin/feov-record", and the tool's own board is the ONLY source of truth for status.
Routing around it into markdown is the failure this contract exists to prevent.
THIS RUN'S BENCH HOLDINGS BIND HOW YOU READ THE RECORD, ITS RULINGS AND ORDERS WHAT YOU DO; each is on your work list, and the board, not a holding, says where a gap stands.
One command's page prints at "/opt/feov/bin/feov-record" --seat-id blue-lane-3 <command> --help.
The projection names, this prompt's words for a concept, and the command that writes it are three different vocabularies and they do not always agree.
The help alone is authoritative; this prompt names the JOB, never the flags.
EVERY READ IS A PROJECTION OF THE RECORD, never a file you open.
The .md files under the run directory are for a HUMAN to verify against; one read instead of the projection is a snapshot of a record that has moved.
YOUR ARGUMENT IS PART OF EVERY ACT, and the RECORD renders it — what you argue to the bench in the transcript, your grounds beside the acts on the board.
The transcript is your case to the bench, which reads it when it rules, and to the reader of the run; the grounds on the board are what the other party's seats read and answer.
NEVER the research report: that document is addressed to a reader of the subject and carries no argument about the run.
And write the SUBSTANCE — what it rests on, and what answers it — not a label for what you did — the record already holds every act of this sitting, in order, so an account of the verbs you ran narrates what the record reconstructs.
An act stands as filed, so read before you record.
One you got wrong stays on the record and is answered by another act: the page for the act names which, and so does the refusal of a second where a sitting holds one.
